the Spanish Inquisition has a certain  reputation it is seen as a bastion of  near indiscriminate torture and  corruption and of brutal repressions of  Jews and Muslims to put it bluntly it's  an institution with a bad reputation but  just how bad was the Spanish Inquisition  really where they really as terrible as  they were made out to be or resist the  result of historical disinformation  now obviously the Spanish Inquisition  was a long-lived body that operated  differently in different areas and was  also reformed several times the way it  did things were subject to change  depending on circumstances and as such  we're going to focus on the period  during the reign of King Philippe ii  just to narrow things down and the  reason why is that this is the period in  which the inquisition was said to be at  its worst before continuing there's  something that does need to be said  though most people expected the Spanish  Inquisition because when a person was  being prosecuted they would give you  roughly a mumps notice so the Spanish  Inquisition was tasked with one thing to  rid Spain of any threats to the Catholic  faith the primary crime they were  looking for was of course heresy  although they did investigate things  like witchcraft so at the time the  accusation of witchcraft in the  practicing of magic was a fairly common  one whilst many people at the time did  believe in witchcraft it's important to  note that the Catholic Church and by  extension the Spanish Inquisition were  very skeptical  the majority of witchcraft cases ended  with acquittal and those who were found  guilty of making false claims were often  fined for those who would unknowingly  committed acts of heresy or claim to be  healers or somesuch their punishment  wasn't death but public penance although  if you were found guilty of being a  witch  you'd be tortured and killed but what  about the Inquisition's main focus  what about heretics well the Inquisition  had been founded to confirm the  conversion of mariscos and conversos who  were former Muslims and Jews who had  been commanded to convert or leave Spain  in the late 15th century these people  were often harassed by the Inquisition  and in many cases they were expected to  pay bribes in order to be left alone so  what if someone was caught with non  Catholic religious texts or word god  forbid a protestant for a start they  certainly be tortured although the  inquisition actually did this less than  secular authorities this would go on  until you confessed or until an  Inquisitor decided on whether or not you  were guilty foreign traders who were  Protestants were often tortured before  being fined and then expelled from Spain  these people then wrote of their ordeals  which were widely disseminated across  the Protestant world hence the  Inquisition's reputation for those who  were guilty didn't repent or couldn't  pay their way out the punishment was  after  death fire burning you should be aware  though that there weren't many of these  and only about 2% of all trials ended  with an execution so to conclude by  modern standards the Spanish Inquisition  was pretty brutal it was driven by a  near rabid hatred of other religions it  was corrupt in its demands for bribes  and it was responsible for widespread  torture and executions by medieval  standards they were often much better  than the alternative they required  evidence for trials they were skeptical  of any accusations and their torture was  often less severe and the Spanish  Inquisition was one of the first  institutions to dismiss confessions  obtained by torture so whilst they were  bad for the time they weren't that bad  compared to other inquisitions  especially the Roman Inquisition they  were quite lenient I hope you enjoyed  this episode and thank you for watching  with the special thanks to my patreon  supporters James Bissonnette calling  Castleman Danny Maloney Marvin Castle  Rob Waterhouse John BJ's Mo Aaron the  white Michael Reynolds James Castaneda  Gustav Swan Marcus Ozma Jordan Longley  James Castaneda Gustav Swan Marcus Ozma  Jordan Longley Gareth Turner mr. show  Rashid Ali spinning three plates filled  - oink oink David Silverman Izzy  Winston Hayward Maggie packs Kowski Lexi  Schwinn Spencer Lightfoot Kelly  moneymaker  Robert Wetzel Skye Chappell and Anthony  Beckett
 

